{% if deployment == "main" -%}
Battuta {{ tag }} install instructions: Oracle Ampere (main machine)
==================================================================

Bundle:        main (CoS, Manager, general workers, memory, artifacts, speech, documents)
Platform:      Oracle Linux 9 on AArch64 (OCI Ampere A1)
{%- else -%}
Battuta {{ tag }} install instructions: Raspberry Pi (influencer machine)
========================================================================

Bundle:        influencer (one influencer worker)
Platform:      Raspberry Pi OS 13 (Debian trixie) on 64-bit ARM (AArch64)
{%- endif %}
Tool version:  {{ tool_version }}
Release page:  {{ release_url }}
Repository:    {{ repo_url }}

Run these steps as the ordinary bot user. Do not use sudo. Stop on any failed
command. Follow RELEASE.md in the archive for the detailed setup; it is the
authoritative guide for the setup steps in sections 4 to 6 below.

1. Download
-----------

Download into a new, empty directory outside the installation path.

  Archive:            {{ release_url }}/{{ archive_name }}
  Archive checksum:   {{ release_url }}/{{ archive_name }}.sha256
  Archive SHA-256:    {{ archive_sha256 }}
  Wheel checksums:    {{ release_url }}/python-tools-SHA256SUMS
  Tool version file:  {{ release_url }}/python-tools-version.txt
{% for wheel in wheels %}
  {{ wheel.package }}:
    {{ wheel.url }}
    SHA-256: {{ wheel.sha256 }}
{% endfor %}
{%- if deployment != "main" %}
The influencer bundle does not use `battuta-artifacts`, `battuta-memory`,
`battuta-speech`, or `battuta-doc`.
{% endif %}

2. Verify checksums
-------------------

Every check must report OK. Never extract or install an unverified download.

  version={{ tag }}
  platform={{ platform }}
  deployment={{ deployment }}
  archive="{{ archive_name }}"
  install_path="$HOME/battuta/latest"
  sha256sum -c "$archive.sha256"
  sha256sum -c python-tools-SHA256SUMS
  tool_version="$(cat python-tools-version.txt)"

3. Install or upgrade the Python tools
--------------------------------------

Install the wheels for this bundle. Each wheel needs its own tool
installation so its command is on PATH. `--force` replaces an earlier version
of the same tool, which is how upgrades are applied.

{% for wheel in wheels %}
  uv tool install --managed-python --python 3.12 --no-build --force "./{{ wheel.filename }}"
{% endfor %}
  export PATH="$(uv tool dir --bin):$PATH"
{%- for wheel in wheels %}
{%- if wheel.package == "battuta-project" %}
  command -v battuta-project >/dev/null
{%- else %}
  {{ wheel.command }} --help >/dev/null
{%- endif %}
{%- endfor %}

`battuta-project` needs `LINEAR_API_TOKEN` even for `--help`, so it is only
located here; RELEASE.md step 4 runs it after loading the private environment.

4. Extract the release and read RELEASE.md
------------------------------------------

Unpack the archive to read the guide; the installation is extracted separately.

  mkdir release && tar -xzf "$archive" -C release
  less release/RELEASE.md

The installation path is always `~/battuta/latest`; do not ask the owner for
another path. For a fresh install, `latest` must not exist yet. For an upgrade,
follow section 5 first. Never clear ~/.battuta or service data.
{%- if deployment != "main" %}

ADB is required on this machine: the owner provides an authorized Android device
with USB debugging, and ADBKeyboard for Unicode input. Confirm `command -v adb`
succeeds. Device readiness is checked on launch, not during setup.
{%- endif %}

Run the prerequisite check in RELEASE.md step 1, then follow RELEASE.md from
step 3 through step 6 with the same `archive`, `deployment`, and `install_path`.
Sections 1 to 3 above cover the downloads and RELEASE.md step 2, so start at
RELEASE.md step 3 with `battuta-packaging extract-bots`.

5. Upgrade
----------

After owner approval, stop the services and rename the active release to its
tag as described in RELEASE.md "Layout and ownership", then repeat sections 1
to 4 with the same `install_path`. Existing `~/.battuta` configuration and
sessions are kept. To roll back, rename the releases back the same way.

6. Launch the server
--------------------

Launch is an owner decision. After setup validation passes, generate the
services and review the printed activation command, which covers
{%- if deployment == "main" %} memory,
artifacts, speech, documents, CoS, Manager, and the selected workers.
{%- else %} only the
influencer worker.
{%- endif %} Activate it only with owner approval.

  battuta-packaging install-services --root "$install_path" \
    --env-file "$HOME/.battuta/.env.prod" --node "$node" --deployment "$deployment"

